        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
          How Car Leasing Works
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          From picking your initial payment to handing the keys back at the end, here's everything you need to know about leasing a car.
        </p>

        <hr class="my-8 border-slate-100">

        <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Leasing vs. buying outright</h2>
          <p>
            When you lease a car, you're paying for the use of the vehicle over an agreed term rather than buying it outright. Because you aren't paying off the entire value of the car—and you don't own it at the end—monthly payments are often lower than traditional finance options for a brand-new model.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">The 3 main choices that shape your quote</h2>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Contract length:</strong> Longer contracts usually spread the cost and lower monthly payments, while shorter contracts let you change cars sooner.</li>
            <li><strong>Annual mileage:</strong> Be honest about how far you drive each year. Picking a mileage allowance that matches your real habits avoids end-of-lease charges.</li>
            <li><strong>Initial payment:</strong> Often shown as a multiple of your monthly rental (such as 1, 3, 6, 9, or 12 months). Putting more down upfront lowers your ongoing monthly payment.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">What happens at the end of your lease?</h2>
          <p>
            Once your term finishes, you simply return the car after a standard end-of-contract inspection (fair wear and tear is expected—just keep it tidy and service it on schedule). From there, you're free to lease another car or walk away.
          </p>
        </div>

